Here's a simple C program that takes command-line arguments and converts them into a single string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *input = malloc(sizeof(char) * (argc + 1));

    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(input, argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) {
            strcat(input, " ");
        }
    }

    printf("Input string: %s\n", input);

    free(input);

    return 0;
}
```

To use this program, save it as `convert_argv.c`, compile it with `gcc convert_argv.c -o convert_argv`, and then run it with the desired command-line arguments:

```bash
./convert_argv arg1 arg2 arg3
```

The program will print the input string, which is a concatenation of all the command-line arguments separated by spaces.

